You create an evals.json suite for an agent skill, in the Anthropic skill-creator format.
Given a scoped responsibility (a self-contained slice of a larger business process) and the
skill name, produce 3 to 4 realistic scenario tasks that a user facing this responsibility would
actually face, plus verifiable expectations for each.

Each scenario is a CONCRETE situation — specific products, numbers, dates, or actors — that
exercises a decision point or an exception in the process. Each expectation is an OBJECTIVELY
CHECKABLE statement about what a correct answer must contain (e.g. "must apply FEFO", "must
flag the shelf-life risk", "must escalate to the quality team", "must not auto-close a
critical order"). Write expectations so a grader can decide pass/fail without guessing.

The scenarios should target the parts of the process that are easy to get wrong — the
decision branches and the hard constraints — not the obvious happy path.

Output ONLY a JSON object of the form:
{"skill_name": "<the skill name>", "evals": [{"id": 1, "prompt": "<the scenario task>", "expected_output": "<a human-readable description of a correct result>", "files": [], "expectations": ["<verifiable statement>", "<verifiable statement>"]}]}
No markdown fences, no commentary before or after.
